## What happened

In **.NET 11**, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) now throws at runtime on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected — DSA still works there.

## How to fix it

Since you need to **verify legacy DSA signatures**, you have two options:

### Option A: Use a platform guard (if you must keep DSA for Windows/Linux)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA is not supported on macOS in .NET 11+.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option B: Migrate away from DSA entirely (recommended)

Replace DSA with **ECDSA** or **RSA** across all platforms. This requires re-signing existing data with the new algorithm:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

> **If you only need to verify old signatures** (not create new ones) and must support macOS, consider performing DSA verification on a Windows/Linux backend service and exposing it as an API, since DSA still works on those platforms.
